--- a/CHANGELOG.md
+++ b/CHANGELOG.md
@@ -11,6 +11,8 @@ Versioning: [Semantic Versioning](https://semver.org/spec/v2.0.0.html)
 
 ### Added
 
+- **Device-key directory `POST /_matrix/client/v3/keys/claim`, `GET /_matrix/client/v3/keys/changes` and `device_lists` in `/sync` (story device-key-directory, epic e2ee-client-support; migration 000060)**: one-time keys are handed out exactly once in upload order (one set based `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` statement), the fallback key is returned without being consumed and marked used, unreachable targets are left out (`failures` is always empty, no existence oracle, equal statement counts), a claim reaches own devices and users who share a joined room, limited to 1000 targets per request and by token buckets (30 per minute, burst 10 per caller; 10 per hour, burst 5 per caller and target device; node-local, N x in a cluster) checked before the claim; `/keys/changes` and the `device_lists` of `/sync` report peers whose device or cross-signing keys changed, users who newly share a room (`changed`) and users who share none any more (`left`) from a commit ordered change stream (`device_list_changes`, position as the last sync token group `.d<n>`); `device_one_time_keys_count` always has `signed_curve25519` and `device_unused_fallback_key_types` is per device (a used fallback key of a second device is no longer masked); GDPR erasure deletes the stream rows; key material and tokens are never logged.
+
 - **To-device messaging `PUT /_matrix/client/v3/sendToDevice/{eventType}/{txnId}` and `to_device.events` in `/sync` (story to-device-messaging, epic e2ee-client-support; migration 000059)**: device verification (SAS between your own devices) and key requests work. An OPAQUE per-device queue in `to_device_messages` / `to_device_txns` (the server never decrypts, interprets or logs a content; both tables are deleted by the GDPR erasure, which also deletes the messages the erased user SENT), acknowledged by the `since` token of the next sync (a retry with the older token delivers again, nothing is lost), idempotent per (user, device, event type, txn id) in the database, a new message wakes the long poll of exactly its target device (`pg`, cluster wide). **Client-visible consequence:** the recipients are the sender's own devices and users the sender shares a JOINED room with (both sides `join`; an invitation opens no send path); every other target (no shared joined room, unknown user or device, remote user) is skipped silently with the same `200 {}` (no existence oracle), so a user you share no joined room with cannot be reached (Element and FluffyChat verify other users in the room, not through to-device messages). Limits: 64 KiB body and 50 recipient entries per request (`413 M_TOO_LARGE`), 100 targets and 1 MiB of rows per request, 500 messages / 8 MiB per device, 2000 messages / 32 MiB per user, per sender 100 per device and 200 messages / 4 MiB per recipient user, 100 messages / 1 MiB per sync response, 7 days TTL (Oban cron `Nebu.Workers.ToDevicePurgeWorker`); a full queue evicts the OLDEST messages (fairly: the sender with the largest share first) and never tells the sender; 60 requests per minute (burst 30) per user, a byte-cost bucket of 4 MiB per minute (burst 8 MiB) per sender (charged after the body is parsed; only the request-rate limit and the send slot come before the body is read), per sender 4000 messages / 16 MiB over all recipients and 1 MiB per recipient device, at most 4 sends per user and 6 per node in flight, 2 s statement timeout (node-local, `429` before the body is read); the soft eviction of full queues runs in a second transaction after the commit (a failed or timed-out eviction never fails the send); a delivery fault answers `/sync` with an empty `to_device` instead of an error. Rooms stay unencrypted (ADR-011). Operators: run migration 000059 with the release; rolling back discards every queued, not yet acknowledged message.
 
 - **Server-side key backup `/_matrix/client/v3/room_keys/**` (story server-side-key-backup, epic e2ee-client-support; migration 000058)**: Element's "chat recovery" and secret-storage bootstrap work. `POST|GET /room_keys/version`, `GET|PUT|DELETE /room_keys/version/{version}` and `PUT|GET|DELETE /room_keys/keys[/{roomId}[/{sessionId}]]` replace the former acceptance stub (a constant `200 {"version":"1"}` that stored nothing): an OPAQUE per-user store (tables `room_key_backup_versions` and `room_key_backup_sessions`; the server never decrypts or logs a blob, ADR-011 stays: no E2EE room is enabled), the spec's merge rule for a re-uploaded session, `etag` and `count` from the database, `403 M_WRONG_ROOM_KEYS_VERSION` with `current_version`, idempotent `DELETE` with a soft-delete marker (versions are never reused). Limits: 10 versions per user (the 11th is `403 M_FORBIDDEN`), 1000 version rows per user including the markers, 100 000 sessions per version, 8 KiB per `session_data`, 16 KiB per `auth_data`, 5 MiB per request body (read only after the authentication), a per-user storage budget of 64 MiB over all live versions (every session row costs its ids and 128 bytes besides its data; `403 M_FORBIDDEN`); writes are rate limited per user (60 per minute, burst 5), the two bulk reads (`GET /keys`, `GET /keys/{room}`) have their own bucket (30 per minute, burst 3) and at most one is in flight per user and two per node, `GET /version` has a light bucket (120 per minute, burst 30), a writer that finds the per-user lock busy answers `429 M_LIMIT_EXCEEDED` at once. The GDPR erasure deletes all versions and sessions of the user. Operators: run migration 000058 with the release; rolling back discards every user's key backup.
