diff --git a/docs/architecture/07-deployment.md b/docs/architecture/07-deployment.md
index da2bc63e..a73d3b5b 100644
--- a/docs/architecture/07-deployment.md
+++ b/docs/architecture/07-deployment.md
@@ -2077,3 +2077,8 @@ _Source: Story 13-11 staged changes - `.gitlab-ci.yml`, `Makefile`, `deploy/helm
 
 `PUT /_matrix/client/v3/sendToDevice/{eventType}/{txnId}` and `to_device.events` of `/sync` queue opaque message contents per (user, device) in the tables `to_device_messages` and `to_device_txns`. Nothing to configure: the migration creates the tables, the runtime role gets its grants there. Operator facts: (1) the queue is database state (every node answers the same, a restart or a rolling deploy loses no message; a message that was delivered but not yet acknowledged by the next `since` token is delivered again); (2) the Oban cron job `Nebu.Workers.ToDevicePurgeWorker` (hourly, queue `oauth_cleanup`) deletes messages older than 7 days and idempotency records older than 24 hours; delivery never returns an expired message, so a stopped job only keeps rows longer; (3) a device removal (`DELETE /devices`, `/delete_devices`, logout, logout-all, the Admin UI revoke) deletes the queue and the records of the device, so a mass logout (for example the maintenance procedure above) discards every queued message and clients simply retry; (4) the per-user rate limit of the send (60 per minute, burst 30) and the byte-cost bucket (4 MiB per minute, burst 8 MiB per sender) are node-local (they live in the memory of each node): with N nodes the effective limit is up to N x the documented limit; at most 6 sends per node are in flight and every statement of a send or delivery is cut after 2 s; a send is two transactions (the rows, then the soft eviction in a second transaction after the commit), so the caps are momentarily soft and a failed eviction leaves the excess until an eviction succeeds (the next send to that queue) or the 7-day purge; (5) the GDPR sweep above deletes the queue, the records and the messages SENT by erased users; the same statement for one user is `DELETE FROM to_device_messages WHERE sender IN (...)` (the messages are queued in other users' devices). The limits (500 messages and 8 MiB per device, 2000 messages and 32 MiB per user, per sender and recipient device 100 messages and 1 MiB, per sender and recipient user 200 messages and 4 MiB, per sender over all recipients 4000 messages and 16 MiB (a soft cap: 16 MiB plus the in-flight fan-out, concurrent sends and rows locked by another transaction can leave a sender above it until its next send, about 4N MiB plus the locked rows), 100 targets per request) are application settings `:nebu, :to_device_*` (defaults in 08-concepts "To-device messaging"); a full queue evicts its oldest messages and logs the number only. Worst-case database footprint: a sender holds at most 16 MiB (4000 messages) over all its recipients, a recipient user at most 32 MiB, so the queue is bounded by (users that share a joined room with a sender) x 32 MiB per recipient and 16 MiB per sender, whichever is smaller per pair; plan the database for the per-sender bound times the number of active senders. Operator notes: the queue positions come from identity / sequence columns, after a restore or a manual sequence reset set `to_device_delivery_seq` and the `stream_pos` identity above their maximum or a client presents positions from the future; the purge job scans by `ctid` batches (housekeeping, delivery never returns an expired message) and shares the Oban queue `oauth_cleanup` with the OAuth cleanup jobs.
 
+### Device-key directory operations (story device-key-directory, epic e2ee-client-support; migration 000060)
+
+`POST /_matrix/client/v3/keys/claim`, `GET /_matrix/client/v3/keys/changes` and the `device_lists` of `/sync` use the new table `device_list_changes` and the existing key tables. Nothing to configure: the migration creates the table, the two triggers and one nullable column (`device_fallback_keys.key_id`), the runtime role gets its grants there. Operator facts: (1) the stream is database state (every node answers the same, a restart or a rolling deploy loses nothing; an old node that receives a token with the new `.d<n>` group answers an initial sync, an old token on a new node is answered conservatively with every shared user once); (2) the migration takes a brief lock on `room_members` for the trigger (run it when no long transaction is open on it); a position is drawn at COMMIT under ONE global advisory lock, held only while the commit finishes, so key and membership writes wait for each other's commit for an instant (they never wait while a client thinks); (3) the table keeps the LATEST change per user and room (bounded by users and memberships), there is nothing to prune, and a down / up cycle loses the history: clients then get the conservative answer once; (4) the claim buckets (30 requests per minute, burst 10 per caller; 10 per hour, burst 5 per caller, target user and target device) are node-local (they live in the memory of each node): with N nodes the effective limit is up to N x the documented limit; the per-target buckets are keyed by the caller's request, so one account can create up to 1000 buckets per request, the idle sweep (one hour) bounds the memory; (5) the GDPR sweep above deletes the stream rows of erased users (`DELETE FROM device_list_changes WHERE user_id IN (...)`), the claim itself keeps no state besides the key tables and the node-local buckets; (6) logging: key material, tokens and claim bodies are never logged (the request logger captures no body of `keys/claim` and `keys/upload` and only the parameter names of `keys/changes`).
+
+
diff --git a/docs/architecture/08-concepts.md b/docs/architecture/08-concepts.md
index 84aa0323..7e345288 100644
--- a/docs/architecture/08-concepts.md
+++ b/docs/architecture/08-concepts.md
@@ -1431,13 +1431,31 @@ applies when the event grows, so a tag can still be deleted after an operator lo
 
 *Client compatibility.* Verification and key requests work between your own devices and with any user you share a joined room with (an invitation does not count); a user you share no joined room with cannot be reached. Element and FluffyChat verify other users in the room, not through to-device messages, so the restriction costs them nothing.
 
+### Device-key directory (story device-key-directory)
+
+`POST /_matrix/client/v3/keys/claim`, `GET /_matrix/client/v3/keys/changes` and the E2E members of `GET /sync` (`device_one_time_keys_count`, `device_unused_fallback_key_types`, `device_lists`; Matrix CS v1.18) complete the key directory next to `/keys/upload` and `/keys/query`. They serve public key material and device-list metadata only: ADR-011 stays (no E2EE room is enabled, `m.room.encryption` is still rejected). Modules: `Nebu.Matrix.HttpKeyDirectory`, `Nebu.Matrix.KeyClaimStore`, `Nebu.Matrix.DeviceListStore` (SQL inline per ADR-018, no domain model); state is PostgreSQL only (ADR-020, cluster safe): the table `device_list_changes` (migration 000060) and the existing `device_one_time_keys` / `device_fallback_keys`.
+
+*Claim.* Exactly once: ONE set based statement takes the oldest unclaimed one-time key of every target with `UPDATE ... WHERE ctid = ANY(...)` over a `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` selection (a key another claim holds is skipped, never waited for, never handed out twice; a SELECT followed by an UPDATE would hand a key out twice); keys go out in upload order (`created_at`); the stored key object is returned verbatim. No one-time key left: the fallback key of that algorithm is returned, NOT consumed but marked `used` (its algorithm leaves `device_unused_fallback_key_types` of THAT device). The fallback key is stored with its key id (`device_fallback_keys.key_id`, `<algorithm>:fallback` for a row from before the migration). The answer is `{"one_time_keys": ..., "failures": {}}`: a target that yields nothing is LEFT OUT and `failures` is always empty (the spec keys it by server name, Nebu has no federation); `timeout` is ignored. Errors: `400 M_NOT_JSON`, `400 M_BAD_JSON` (`one_time_keys` missing or not an object, an entry that is not an object, an algorithm that is not a string), `400 M_INVALID_PARAM` (more than 1000 targets, before any bucket is charged), `429 M_LIMIT_EXCEEDED` with `retry_after_ms`.
+
+*Restriction and no oracle.* The caller may claim its own devices and the devices of users who share a room with it where BOTH sides are `join` (the rule of `sendToDevice`), evaluated at request time inside the statement; an invitation opens no path; a deactivated or erased user is neither caller nor target. This is stricter than the spec (a claim consumes a key). Unknown users and devices, keyless devices, users without a shared room, invite-only shares, remote users and malformed ids are answered BYTE-IDENTICALLY and run the SAME number of statements (one claim statement, no early return, no statement per target), pinned by counting.
+
+*Rate limits.* Token buckets through `Nebu.Web.Plugs.RateLimit.take_all/1`, checked all-or-nothing BEFORE the claim statement: per caller 30 requests per minute, burst 10 (requests, not targets), and per (caller, target user, target device) 10 per hour, burst 5, for one-time and fallback claims alike and charged for EVERY valid target whatever its state (a limit that depended on the target would be an existence oracle). Any bucket over its limit: `429 M_LIMIT_EXCEEDED` for the whole request, nothing consumed, nothing charged. The buckets are node-local: with N nodes up to N x the documented limit (07-deployment); the per-target buckets are keyed by caller chosen ids (11-risks).
+
+*Changes.* `GET /keys/changes?from=&to=`: both are sync `next_batch` tokens; `400 M_MISSING_PARAM` when one is missing or empty, `400 M_INVALID_PARAM` when one is malformed; a well-formed token without a device-list position (an "expired" token) is answered conservatively: every user the caller shares a joined room with is `changed`. `changed` and `left` are always lists. A user is reported only through the caller's OWN shared rooms (both sides `join`) or when it is the caller itself: key changes (identity keys new or different, a device with keys removed or renamed, cross-signing keys), users who newly share a joined room, `left` = users who share no joined room any more after a leave in a room both were in.
+
+*The stream.* `device_list_changes (user_id, room_id, kind, pos)` keeps the LATEST change per (user, room): kind `keys` (written by `DeviceKeyStore` and `CrossSigningKeyStore.put_keys/2`), `join` / `leave` (a trigger on `room_members`, so every writer is covered). `pos` comes from `sync_global_pos_seq` and is assigned AT COMMIT by a deferred constraint trigger under one global advisory lock: visible positions are commit ordered, so a reader that has seen position M never misses a change that was written earlier and commits later. The position is the LAST group of the sync token: `<core>.<account data pos>[.<to-device pos>].d<device-list pos>`; every response carries it, an incremental answer carries the highest position it reported, and a token without the group is "unknown" (conservative answer). A rolling deploy is safe: an old node does not recognise the group and answers an initial sync, old tokens keep working. A forged position can only hide the caller's own future lists.
+
+*Sync members.* `device_one_time_keys_count` is per device and ALWAYS contains `signed_curve25519` (0 when none); `device_unused_fallback_key_types` is ALWAYS a list and PER DEVICE (it was a user aggregate: a second device's used fallback key was masked by the first device's unused one); a device-less request (an unbound raw IdP JWT) keeps the legacy per-user aggregates. `device_lists` (`changed`, `left`, both lists): an incremental sync lists the changes above, the caller itself for its own devices; an initial sync lists no peer; a fallback of a request that carried a `since` lists every shared user. A pending change ends a long poll at once; a change that arrives during a long poll is delivered with the next response (no wake-up).
+
+*Logging and erasure.* Key material, tokens, claim bodies and the sync tokens of `from` / `to` are never logged (the request logger captures no body of `keys/claim` and `keys/upload`, only parameter names of `keys/changes`; a failing statement logs its kind only). `device_list_changes` is in `UserDeletion.erased_tables/0` (the runbook sweep names it).
+
 ### GDPR erasure inventory (story gdpr-erasure-global-data)
 
 GDPR erasure (`Nebu.Compliance.UserDeletion.delete_user_keys/3`) physically deletes every row of personal data that is stored under the user in a per-user table, in the SAME transaction, in this order: the `deletion_in_progress` marker, the private keys set to NULL, the row locks and the advisory lock (below), the per-user deletes (then a check that no `oauth_codes` row is left), the profile anonymisation, and `keys_deleted`; one failing step rolls everything back and writes the `user_keys_deletion_attempted` audit row, whose `error_detail` names the failing step (`step=<name>`, a code atom, never data). *Lock order:* the transaction first locks the user's `uia_sessions` rows and `cross_signing_keys` row (`FOR UPDATE`) and only THEN takes the per-user `sync_global` advisory lock of `AccountDataStore.put/3`, `CrossSigningKeyStore.put_keys/2` and the signature merge: the order of the cross-signing reset writer (uia row, cross-signing row, advisory lock), so a writer in flight is serialised with the erasure and the two do not deadlock (one narrow exception, below). Those three writers refuse a user whose `deletion_status` is set (`{:error, :user_erased}`, answered `403 M_USER_DEACTIVATED`). The key backup writers `RoomKeyBackupStore.create_version/3` and `RoomKeyBackupStore.put_sessions/3` take the same per-user `sync_global` lock and refuse an erased user the same way (they run after the lock, so a write in flight is serialised with the erasure; the lock is a try-lock, a busy lock is a 429, see "Server-side key backup"). The cross-signing writers (both branches of `device_signing/upload` and the signature merge) decide on the stored keys only through `CrossSigningKeyStore.lock_keys/1`, in the same order: the `cross_signing_keys` row lock, then the `sync_global` advisory lock, and only THEN the read (row -> advisory -> read; story cross-signing-upload-races), so two concurrent first uploads store one key set and a signature merge never writes back a stale merge base. The exception is the absent-then-present window (accepted residual): when the row lock of a signature merge found no row and a first upload commits the row before the merge gets the advisory lock, an erasure that locks the new row and waits for the advisory lock can form a cycle with the merge's write, which only now waits for that row; PostgreSQL aborts one side (`40P01`: the erasure fails closed, or the merge answers 500). A first upload has the same window when the row committed in between has a NULL master (a partial upload): its read sees no master and its upsert takes the row lock only after the advisory lock (pre-existing; the erasure fails closed, or the upload answers 500). Three actors of one user within milliseconds; closing it would need the advisory lock first everywhere, which the story rejected. Events authored by the user are retained (room history preservation, recital 65; immutable signed event log, the sender is the pseudonym); the profile is anonymised (`displayname = 'Deleted User'`, `avatar_url = NULL`, `users.anonymized_at`); admin deactivation keeps all of it (reversible). A second erasure answers `:conflict` and changes nothing.
 
 **The erasure is terminal for authentication.** The last step also sets `users.is_active = false` and NULLs the `users` PII columns (display name, avatar and email ciphertexts: the display name and avatar are encrypted with the server-wide key, so the key deletion does not shred them; also `last_seen_at` and `deactivation_reason`, admin free text that may describe the person). Every authentication gate (`TokenValidator`, `OAuthSessionStore.user_active/1`, `Nebu.Matrix.active?/1`, the account pages, `invitable_local_users`) treats `deletion_status IS NOT NULL` as deactivated, so neither a fresh IdP token, an SSO login, an OAuth authorization nor an admin reactivation revives the account, and `Profile.DB.upsert_profile/3` writes nothing for an erased user. A bearer (an unbound raw IdP JWT included) that the node-local provisioning cache of the OIDC plug (60 s TTL) already confirmed is still accepted on that node until the entry expires: new requests, not only one in flight, for up to 60 s, the same bound as for an admin deactivation. In that window it can still write the tables WITHOUT a guard (device keys, receipts, room account data, the lazy device row with its IP); the profile PUT, global account data, cross-signing, the signature merge and the sync persist are refused. The eviction of the cache entry on every node (a broadcast over `:pg` on erasure and deactivation) is the proper fix, recorded in follow-up `gn`. Admin UI access of an erased administrator follows the IdP admin group claim, not the erasure: remove the person from the IdP admin group. `Nebu.Admin.DB.set_is_active/2` refuses to reactivate an erased user (`:erased`, answered as a failed precondition) and the admin user suggestions never offer one.
 
-**Erased by the transaction** (`UserDeletion.erased_tables/0`, the list the deleting code and the guard test share): `user_room_account_data`, `account_data`, `cross_signing_keys`, `device_keys`, `device_one_time_keys`, `device_fallback_keys`, `forgotten_rooms`, `auto_join_opt_outs`, `read_receipts`, `uia_sessions`, `oauth_account_sessions`, `oauth_consent_requests`, `oauth_codes`, `room_key_backup_sessions`, `room_key_backup_versions` (the server-side key backup of story server-side-key-backup, migration 000058: every version row, the soft-delete markers included, and every session row). `to_device_messages`, `to_device_txns` (the to-device queue and its idempotency records of story to-device-messaging, migration 000059: every message queued FOR the user, every record, and, by one more statement on `sender`, every message the user SENT that waits in another user's queue). `ToDeviceStore.send` takes its OWN fence key `to_device_erasure|<user>` (the erasure takes it exclusively inside the `sync_global` writers' lock set; it is not the `sync_global` key itself, so those writers do not make a message skip): it takes the user's lock SHARED as a try-lock (a busy lock is a 429 for the sender and a silent skip for a recipient) and refuses an erased sender with `403 M_USER_DEACTIVATED`, so a send in flight never re-creates rows after the erasure. (`oauth_codes` needs the delete policy of migration 000055.)
+**Erased by the transaction** (`UserDeletion.erased_tables/0`, the list the deleting code and the guard test share): `user_room_account_data`, `account_data`, `cross_signing_keys`, `device_keys`, `device_one_time_keys`, `device_fallback_keys`, `forgotten_rooms`, `auto_join_opt_outs`, `read_receipts`, `uia_sessions`, `oauth_account_sessions`, `oauth_consent_requests`, `oauth_codes`, `room_key_backup_sessions`, `room_key_backup_versions` (the server-side key backup of story server-side-key-backup, migration 000058: every version row, the soft-delete markers included, and every session row). `device_list_changes` (the device-list change stream of story device-key-directory, migration 000060: the key changes and the membership changes of the user, deleted by `user_id`). `to_device_messages`, `to_device_txns` (the to-device queue and its idempotency records of story to-device-messaging, migration 000059: every message queued FOR the user, every record, and, by one more statement on `sender`, every message the user SENT that waits in another user's queue). `ToDeviceStore.send` takes its OWN fence key `to_device_erasure|<user>` (the erasure takes it exclusively inside the `sync_global` writers' lock set; it is not the `sync_global` key itself, so those writers do not make a message skip): it takes the user's lock SHARED as a try-lock (a busy lock is a 429 for the sender and a silent skip for a recipient) and refuses an erased sender with `403 M_USER_DEACTIVATED`, so a send in flight never re-creates rows after the erasure. (`oauth_codes` needs the delete policy of migration 000055.)
 
 **Ended after the commit by the session teardown** (best effort, outside the transaction): `devices`, `matrix_sessions`, `jwt_device_bindings`, `sessions`, `oauth_idp_refresh_leases`, `sync_tokens`, `sync_room_cursors` (the dispatcher also removes the `devices` rows of legacy devices).
 
